\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For functions $f_k$ whose Fourier supports lie in $\delta$-plates of the truncated light cone in $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$, does $\lVert\sum_k f_k\rVert_p\leq C_\varepsilon\delta^{-\alpha(p)-\varepsilon}(\sum_k\lVert f_k\rVert_p^p)^{1/p}$ hold for $p>2(d+1)/(d-1)$, where $\alpha(p)=d(1/2-1/p)-1/2$? Does the stronger mixed-norm version with $(\sum_k\lVert f_k\rVert_p^2)^{1/2}$ and exponent $\beta(p)=(d-1)/4-(d+1)/(2p)$ hold for $p>2+4/(d-1)$? Do the associated Sogge local-smoothing and cone-multiplier estimates hold in their conjectured ranges?
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2008/32}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
For functions $f_k$ whose Fourier supports lie in $\delta$-plates of the truncated light cone in $\mathbb{R}^{d+1}$, does $\lVert\sum_k f_k\rVert_p\leq C_\varepsilon\delta^{-\alpha(p)-\varepsilon}(\sum_k\lVert f_k\rVert_p^p)^{1/p}$ hold for $p>2(d+1)/(d-1)$, where $\alpha(p)=d(1/2-1/p)-1/2$? Does the stronger mixed-norm version with $(\sum_k\lVert f_k\rVert_p^2)^{1/2}$ and exponent $\beta(p)=(d-1)/4-(d+1)/(2p)$ hold for $p>2+4/(d-1)$? Do the associated Sogge local-smoothing and cone-multiplier estimates hold in their conjectured ranges?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30001000 (OWR-2043-005). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Original problem formulation and mathematical context.
\url{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2008/32}.
\end{itemize}
\label{end:30001000}

